            <div xml:id="d316">

                <pb facs="0588" n="472" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg472"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.428/729</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Geddes</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-04-04"><hi rendition="#italic">April 4,
                        1923</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: In your note of the 28th
                    ultimo referring to the Resolution of March 4, 1923, by which the Senate gave
                    its advice and consent to the ratification of the convention for the protection
                    of the halibut fishery of the North Pacific Ocean, you inquired in regard to the
                    meaning that it is intended the words “any other part of Great Britain” shall
                    have as they are used in the Resolution. I note your inquiry whether in the mind
                    of the framers of the Resolution “Great Britain” is intended to be synonymous
                    with the term “British Empire.”</p>
                <p>Senator Lodge, Chairman of the Committee on Foreign Relations of the Senate,
                    informs me that he has conferred with Senator Jones of the State of Washington,
                    who introduced the Resolution in the Senate and that the intention was
                    undoubtedly to cover any part of the British Empire.</p>
                <p>I trust that this information will be of assistance.</p>
                <p>I am [etc]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
